# Core engineering standards

## Testing
- Every function with real logic gets a test that exercises actual behavior, not a mock that always passes.
- Assert on outcomes, not on implementation details like internal call counts.
- Do not write a test that only checks a function does not throw an exception.

## Comments
- Comment why a decision was made, not what the line does.
- Do not restate the function name in a comment above it.
- Delete commented out code. Do not leave it in place.

## Structure
- One function does one job. Extract any function longer than 30 to 40 lines, including nested closures returned by a factory function.
- One file does one job. Split a file once it holds more than one clear responsibility (e.g. CLI argument parsing, persistence, interactive prompts, and orchestration logic each belong in their own module).
- Before adding another responsibility to a file, check its current line count. A file that has crossed roughly 300 lines and keeps growing is a signal to split it, not to keep appending.
- Name booleans with is, has, or should. Name functions as verbs. Name collections as plurals.
- DONT MAKE GIT COMMITS WITHOUT MY CONSENT

## Behavior
- Search the codebase for an existing function or pattern before writing a new one.
- Make the smallest change that solves the problem. Do not refactor unrelated code in the same pass.
- Do not invent APIs, libraries, or function signatures. Check before assuming something exists.
- Never duplicate the same magic number or default (retry limits, timeouts, tolerances) across multiple files. Define it once in `src/config.py` and import it.
